<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    The resource-server half: the RFC 9728 metadata endpoints (E-22/E-23) and the bearer
    middleware that turns a missing or bad access token into a challenge (X-32..X-35).

    THERE IS NO REFERENCE TO Boltway.AuthorizationServer, AND THAT ABSENCE IS THE DESIGN.

    A resource server and an authorization server are separate deployables. The RS-only
    customer takes this package and never installs the AS; a project reference would drag a
    grant store, a signing key ring and a consent pipeline into a process that must not have
    any of them, and would make "these two can be deployed apart" a claim rather than a fact
    about the dependency graph.

    What they do share is the wire format, and that is exactly what the two references below
    are: Primitives fixes WWW-Authenticate quoting, resource identity and ordinal comparison;
    Tokens fixes the access-token profile (typ, alg, aud). Sharing the compiled code is what
    makes an AS/RS disagreement about any of those impossible rather than merely tested.

    Not multi-targeted. docs/DESIGN.md 2 says net8.0;net10.0 for this package; that is a
    packaging decision needing the net8.0 targeting pack in CI and a second matrix leg, and
    neither exists yet. Written here so the gap is visible rather than assumed closed.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <IsPackable>true</IsPackable>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <FrameworkReference Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.App" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Boltway.OAuth.Primitives\Boltway.OAuth.Primitives.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Boltway.OAuth.Tokens\Boltway.OAuth.Tokens.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- Used directly: JsonWebTokenHandler to validate, SecurityKey to hold the verification
         keys. They arrive transitively through Tokens anyway; naming them here means a future
         change to Tokens' dependencies cannot silently remove them from under this code. -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.IdentityModel.JsonWebTokens" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.IdentityModel.Tokens" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.BannedApiAnalyzers">
      <IncludeAssets>runtime; build; native; contentfiles; analyzers; buildtransitive</IncludeAssets>
      <PrivateAssets>all</PrivateAssets>
    </PackageReference>
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    A second enforcement scope for the normalizing members of System.Uri, for the same reason
    Primitives has one and independently of it: the ban list is per-project, so Primitives'
    list does not reach this assembly.

    The value at stake here is the resource identifier. RFC 9728 3.3 requires the `resource`
    member of the metadata document to be byte-identical to the identifier the client inserted
    the well-known suffix into, and 6 forbids Unicode normalization at any point. Every banned
    member below returns a value that has been through at least one normalization, so building
    either the document or the metadata URL out of one produces bytes the client compares and
    rejects — and the failure surfaces on the client as a generic "couldn't connect", with the
    server's own log showing a clean 200.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <AdditionalFiles Include="BannedSymbols.txt" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Boltway.ResourceServer.Tests" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
